<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<score-partwise version="4.0">
  <work><work-title>Engraving fixture: accidental completion rules (R-3 A1-A7, C1-C2)</work-title></work>
  <identification>
    <creator type="arranger">Musicanyya test fixture</creator>
    <rights>CC0-1.0 (test fixture, original work)</rights>
    <encoding><software>Musicanyya</software><encoding-date>2026-09-23</encoding-date></encoding>
  </identification>
  <part-list>
    <score-part id="P1"><part-name>Piano</part-name></score-part>
  </part-list>
  <part id="P1">
  <!--
  Feature 006 fixture: one part, one staff, 4/4 throughout, divisions=4 (quarter=4 division units - every
  note in this file is a plain quarter, or a duration-less grace 16th, so rhythm/beaming is never a
  factor; this file has NO <beam> element anywhere, on purpose, since it is only about R-3 accidentals).
  Key signature starts at D major (<fifths>2</fifths>: F and C are sharp by the key; every other letter
  is natural by the key). "Required" below means A4's rule fires (the written <alter> differs from what
  A2 says is expected); "courtesy" means C2 fires (no sign is otherwise required, but the previous bar
  used a different alteration of that letter). Unless a measure-comment says otherwise, notes have NO
  <accidental> element in this source file - that absence, plus the <alter> actually written, is exactly
  what the engraving-completion module is expected to resolve.

  M1 (A4 required + A6 per-octave scope) - key: D major.
    Note 1, G4, <alter>1</alter>, no accidental in the source: G is natural by the key (expected 0), this
    note's alter (1) differs -> REQUIRED sharp. Sets this bar's (staff 1, G, octave 4) state to +1.
    Note 2, G5, <alter>0</alter>: a DIFFERENT octave of the same letter. Per A6, state is tracked per
    octave, so G5 is unaffected by G4's sharp; alter (0) matches the key (0) -> NO accidental.
    Note 3, G4, <alter>0</alter>: same (staff, G, octave 4) as note 1. The bar state for that exact pitch
    is now +1 (from note 1), this note's alter (0) differs -> REQUIRED natural (a "return to what the key
    signature itself would say" after the sharp).
    Note 4, A4, <alter>0</alter>: unrelated filler, A is natural by the key -> NO accidental.

  M2/M3 (A3 ties do not leak into A2's same-bar state) - key: D major (unchanged).
    M2 notes 1-3 (A4, B4, G4) are unrelated filler, all natural-by-key letters written plain -> NO
    accidental on any of them.
    M2 note 4, C5, <alter>0</alter>, <tie type="start"/>: C is sharp by the key (expected +1), this
    note's alter (0) differs -> REQUIRED natural. Tied into measure 3.
    M3 note 1, C5, <alter>0</alter>, <tie type="stop"/>: the same step/octave/alter as the tie-start ->
    per A3, NO accidental is printed, and this tie-stop does NOT set measure 3's (staff 1, C, octave 5)
    bar state (its alteration is remembered only for C1's courtesy memory, not for A2's same-bar state).
    M3 note 2, D4, unrelated filler -> NO accidental.
    M3 note 3, C5, <alter>0</alter>, NOT tied (a fresh, unrelated event of the identical pitch): because
    the tie-stop in note 1 did not set the bar state (A3), the "expected" value for THIS note falls back
    to the key (C = +1), and alter (0) differs from that -> REQUIRED natural, again, in the very same
    bar as the tie-stop - the point of this pair of measures: a tie's alteration carries only as far as
    A3/C1 say, never further.
    M3 note 4, E4, unrelated filler -> NO accidental.

  M4 (A2 mid-bar key change) - starts in D major, changes key mid-measure.
    Note 1, F4, <alter>1</alter>: F is sharp by the (still D major) key -> matches -> NO accidental. This
    is "a note altered [relative to F-natural] before the change", printed with no sign because it
    already agreed with the key in force at that point.
    Mid-measure <attributes><key><fifths>-1</fifths></key></attributes>: key changes to F major (only
    B is flat by this key; F itself is natural in F major). F is one of the letters whose KEY-IMPLIED
    alteration actually changes between the two keys (D major: F=+1: F major: F=0), so per A2 its bar
    state resets at this key change.
    Note 2, F4, <alter>0</alter>: now F is natural by the (new, F major) key -> matches -> NO accidental.
    This is "the note that was altered before the change reads plainly by the new key with no extra sign
    needed" - completion must add NOTHING here even though the same letter/octave needed no sign for the
    opposite reason four beats earlier.
    Notes 3-4, G4 and A4, unrelated filler (natural in both keys) -> NO accidental.

  M5 (A5 same-position chord, two different alterations) - key resets explicitly to D major
    (<attributes><key><fifths>2</fifths></key></attributes> at the top of this measure, for a clean
    slate independent of measure 4's mid-bar change to F major).
    Beat 1 is a two-note chord at F4: the chord HEAD (no <chord/>), <alter>0</alter> - F is sharp by the
    key (expected +1), alter (0) differs -> REQUIRED natural. The chord MEMBER (<chord/>), also F4 but
    <alter>1</alter> - alone this would exactly match the key (+1) and need nothing, but A5 says: two
    notes at the same position, same step, same octave, different alter, BOTH need their own sign -> this
    note ALSO gets a REQUIRED sharp, purely because of the clash with the chord head.
    Notes 3-5 (G4, A4, B4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental on any of them.

  M6 (A4 double-sharp resolving to a plain sharp) - key: D major.
    Note 1, F4, <alter>2</alter>, WITH <accidental>double-sharp</accidental> ALREADY PRESENT in this
    source file: this is the one deliberately pre-accidented note in this fixture. Per A4, an existing
    <accidental> is left COMPLETELY UNTOUCHED by completion, no matter what - completion must not touch
    this element. It still sets this bar's (staff 1, F, octave 4) state to +2 (whatever the printed sign
    says, per A4).
    Note 2, F4, <alter>1</alter>, no accidental in the source: bar state is +2 (from note 1), this note's
    alter (1) differs -> REQUIRED accidental. Per A4's specific rule ("double sharp -> sharp prints a
    plain sharp"), the inserted sign is a plain sharp, NOT a natural followed by a sharp.
    Notes 3-4 (G4, A4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental.

  M7/M8 (A7 grace notes, and C1/C2 cross-bar courtesy) - key: D major (unchanged).
    M7 grace note (before note 1), E5, <alter>-1</alter>, no accidental in the source: E is natural by
    the key (expected 0), alter (-1) differs -> REQUIRED flat. Per A7, a grace note is an ordinary event
    that happens before its main note, and its effect on the bar's accidental state lasts to the END of
    the bar (not just until its own main note).
    M7 note 1, G5, and note 2, A5: unrelated filler the grace happens to precede -> NO accidental on
    either.
    M7 note 3, E5, <alter>-1</alter>, no accidental in the source: same (staff 1, E, octave 5) as the
    grace, later in the SAME bar. Per A7 the grace's flat is still the bar state here, so expected (-1)
    matches this note's alter (-1) -> NO accidental needed; completion must add NOTHING to this note -
    proof that the grace's alteration, not just its pitch, governs the rest of the bar.
    M7 note 4, B4, unrelated filler -> NO accidental.
    M8 note 1, E4, <alter>0</alter>, no accidental in the source: a DIFFERENT octave of the letter the
    grace altered. Nothing in measure 8 has set (staff 1, E, octave 4) yet, and E is natural by the key,
    so alter (0) matches expected (0) -> A4 does NOT require anything. But per C1, the previous bar
    (measure 7) ended with letter E (any octave, including the grace) at alteration -1, which DIFFERS
    from this note's own correct alteration (0) -> C2 fires: a plain COURTESY natural is added, even
    though nothing strictly required it.
    M8 notes 2-4 (F4 matching the key's sharp, G4, A4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental on any of them.

  M9/M10/M11 (C1 special case: the first bar of a numbered ending looks at the bar before ENDING 1, not
  at the bar physically before it in the file, i.e. not at ending 1 itself) - key: D major (unchanged).
    M9 (the bar immediately before the repeat/ending group - no <barline>/<ending> here) note 1, B4,
    <alter>0</alter>: B is natural by the key -> matches -> NO accidental. This bar ends with letter B at
    alteration 0; that is the value the C1 special case says measure 11 (ending 2's first bar) must use.
    M9 notes 2-4 (C5 matching the key's sharp, D4, E4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental.
    M10 (ending 1: <barline location="left"><ending number="1" type="start"/></barline> before note 1;
    <barline location="right"><ending number="1" type="stop"/><repeat direction="backward"/></barline>
    after note 4) note 1, B4, <alter>-1</alter>, no accidental in the source: B is natural by the key
    (expected 0), alter (-1) differs -> REQUIRED flat. This also makes ending 1's OWN ending alteration
    for letter B equal to -1 - a deliberate "distractor" value, different from measure 9's 0, so that
    using the wrong previous bar for courtesy memory would be observable.
    M10 notes 2-4 (C5 matching the key's sharp, D4, E4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental.
    M11 (ending 2: <barline location="left"><ending number="2" type="start"/></barline> before note 1;
    <barline location="right"><ending number="2" type="stop"/></barline> after note 4) note 1, B4,
    <alter>0</alter>, no accidental in the source: B is natural by the key (expected 0) -> A4 does not
    require anything on its own. C1's special case says this bar's courtesy memory for letter B must come
    from measure 9 (the bar before ending 1), which ended at 0 - the SAME as this note's own correct
    alteration -> C2 does NOT fire either -> completion must add NOTHING to this note. (If an
    implementation incorrectly used ending 1/measure 10's physically-preceding value of -1 instead, it
    would wrongly add a courtesy natural here - that wrong outcome is exactly what this measure is built
    to catch.)
    M11 notes 2-4 (C5 matching the key's sharp, D4, E4), unrelated filler -> NO accidental.
  -->
  <measure number="1">
